*  * @file GIT.STOCK
*  *
*  * GIT.STOCK(REPO, SPATH) — where a fresh account of this release keeps its
*  * VOC, as "<data-path><AM><dict-path>", or "" where the platform has no such
*  * thing to offer.
*  *
*  * WHY THIS IS A FILE OF ITS OWN, and not four lines inside GIT.ADD:
*  *
*  * IFDEF DOES NOT NEST.  An inner ENDIF closes the OUTER guard, and the rest
*  * of the program silently falls out of the build — it still compiles, so the
*  * only symptom is a change that appears to do nothing.  Both callers are
*  * already wrapped whole (GIT.ADD in IFDEF MVX/ELSE, GITUDT.CHECKOUT in
*  * IFNDEF MVX), so ANY guard placed inside them is a nested one.
*  *
*  * And a guard is unavoidable, because GETENV is UniData-only: on UniVerse it
*  * does not return empty, it does not PARSE — the compiler reads GETENV("X")
*  * as a subscripted array and says "Array 'GETENV' never dimensioned".
*  *
*  * Here the guard is the whole body, so it is top-level and nests inside
*  * nothing.  On UniVerse this compiles to a subroutine that answers "", which
*  * is the honest answer there: uv-git builds its own per-flavour baseline.
*  *
*  * The path is the baseline itself, not a copy of it — newacct copies
*  * $UDTHOME/sys/VOC into every new account verbatim ("cp MASTERVOC to VOC"),
*  * so the master IS the stock account, exact for the release running.  See
*  * mv_git#46.
*
      SUBROUTINE GIT.STOCK(REPO, SPATH)
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
      SPATH = ""
$IFDEF UDT
      UH = GETENV("UDTHOME")
      IF UH # "" THEN
         SPATH = UH : "/sys/VOC" : CHAR(254) : UH : "/sys/D_VOC"
      END
$ENDIF
      RETURN
      END
